## 2. Quick start

### 2.1 Open the offline playground

The website is published at <https://tmquan.github.io/VLOnto/>. It also runs from a folder on disk, without a server and without network access:

1. Clone the repository <https://github.com/tmquan/VLOnto> or download it as a ZIP file.
2. Double-click `site/index.html` in the repository folder: it opens the 3D ontology playground as a `file://` URL. The overview of VLOnto, with the key counts of the build, is `site/about.html`.
3. The other pages are the legal-network playground, the SPARQL playground, the dashboard{{ ", the question traces" if tr else "" }}, the ontology documentation, this playbook and the downloads page; section 9 lists every page with its file name.

All scripts, fonts and data are inside the folder; the pages load no external resources. The site is checked with headless Chrome over `file://` and `http://` (`tools/site_check.py`); Safari and Firefox are not machine-tested, so open the site in them before relying on them (section 12).

### 2.2 Get the code and the data

VLOnto is published as code and a website, not as a data release. The GitHub repository <https://github.com/tmquan/VLOnto> holds the build code (`vlonto/`), the corpus pipeline (`pipeline/`), the ontology (`ontology/`), this playbook (`playbook/`) and the website (`site/`), which GitHub Pages serves at <https://tmquan.github.io/VLOnto/>. The data comes from the {{ n_src }} source datasets on Hugging Face (section 3): `python -m vlonto.cli all` builds the knowledge graph from them on your machine (section 5) and writes the parquet tables to `build/kg/` and the RDF dumps to `build/rdf/`. The build reads the processed corpus of the vn-legal project (section 5.1), which `pipeline/` produces from the source datasets (see `pipeline/README.md`). The ontology and the browser subset need no build: the website's downloads page offers them.

