{% for name, s in cfg.sources | dictsort %}{% set _ = rows.append([Md("[`" ~ name ~ "`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/tmquan/" ~ name ~ ")"), s.kind, s.publisher, Md("`" ~ s.revision[:12] ~ "`"), s.licence]) %}{% endfor %}
{{ table(["Dataset", "Content", "Source", "Revision", "Licence"], rows, [25, 20, 27, 14, 14]) }}
{% if deferred %}

The court datasets {% for d in deferred %}[`{{ d }}`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/tmquan/{{ d }}){{ " and " if not loop.last else "" }}{% endfor %} are not read by {{ release }}; they join in VLOnto v2.
{% endif %}

**Processed corpus.** The vn-legal project (the data pipeline of the Vietnamese legal assistant, section 8) downloads the pinned revisions, cleans and scopes the documents, splits them into articles and extracts the validity map from the text of newer documents (validity rules v7). VLOnto reads its outputs instead of re-parsing the sources:

{% set rows = [] %}
{% for k, p in cfg.processed | dictsort %}{% set _ = rows.append([Md("`" ~ k ~ "`"), Md("`" ~ p ~ "`")]) %}{% endfor %}
{{ table(["Input", "File (vn-legal root R)"], rows, [25, 75]) }}

From the vbpl snapshot, {{ num(b.vbpl_inscope_rows) }} documents are in scope (the normative documents kept by the cleaning step of vn-legal); the Q&As, terms and news are read from the source snapshots directly.

![In-scope legal documents by type: number and share of the {{ num(st.documents) }} documents.](figures/doc-types.svg)

**Knowledge-graph build.** `python -m vlonto.cli all` (section 5) resolves every reference to a document, article, clause or point, writes the node and edge tables with the method and evidence of each edge, exports RDF, validates, and writes the website data. {{ num(st.kg_nodes_parquet) }} nodes and {{ num(st.kg_edges_parquet) }} edges are stored in the parquet tables.

**Outputs.** The parquet tables are the primary form (fast, columnar, with provenance columns); the RDF dump states the same facts with the ontology's IRIs ({{ num(st.rdf_triples) }} triples); the core Turtle subset serves the browser; the website and this playbook are generated from the build reports. There is no separate data release: the tables and the full dump are rebuilt from the source datasets, while the GitHub repository and the website carry the code, the ontology, the core subset and this playbook (section 2.2).

**What is deliberately left out.** No article or document bodies, no Q&A or news text, no glossary definitions and no person names are published; titles, article headings (up to 200 characters) and one-sentence evidence quotes (up to 300 characters) are kept as labels. Text stays at the source URL that every record carries (Appendix D).{% if tr %} The website's question traces page (section 9) is the one exception: it shows the published title of each question it traces, with a short English gloss, but not the full question and no answer text.{% endif +%}

## 4. The ontology

VLOnto is an OWL 2 ontology in Turtle (`ontology/vlonto.ttl`, {{ num(o.header.triples) }} triples) with {{ o.by_kind.class }} classes, {{ o.by_kind.object_property }} object properties, {{ o.by_kind.datatype_property }} datatype properties, {{ o.by_kind.annotation_property }} annotation properties and {{ o.schemes | length }} SKOS concept schemes. Every VLOnto term has an English and a Vietnamese label and an English definition (the completeness check of section 6), and {{ o.terms | selectattr("definition_vi") | list | length }} of the {{ o.terms | length }} terms also have a Vietnamese definition. Namespace: `{{ o.header.namespace }}` (prefix `vlo:`); instances: `{{ o.header.instance_base }}` (prefix `vlid:`).

